<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Daniel Webster</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1782">1782</a>–<a href="/years/1852">1852</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Daniel Webster was an American lawyer, statesman and diplomat who represented <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-hampshire">New Hampshire</a> and Massachusetts in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-congress">United States Congress</a> and served twice as secretary of state, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-henry-harrison">William Henry Harrison</a>, John Tyler and Millard Fillmore. He argued over 200 cases before the Supreme Court, winning Dartmouth College v. Woodward, McCulloch v. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a> and Gibbons v. Ogden. With Henry Clay and John C. Calhoun he formed the Great Triumvirate of the Senate. He opposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-jackson">Andrew Jackson</a>'s domestic policies and Calhoun's theory of nullification, and his Second Reply to Hayne of 1830 was long studied by students of oratory.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/daniel-webster.jpg" alt="Daniel Webster" width="330" height="431" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Possibly John Adams Whipple · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Daniel_Webster_Photograph_edited.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Salisbury, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in Marshfield, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.38,-71.72&amp;year=1827" aria-label="Salisbury on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2823 625 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Daniel Webster"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3273 895h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3273 895h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.38,-71.72&amp;year=1827">What was here in 1827? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…that other sentiment, dear to every true American heart,—Liberty and Union, now and for ever, one and inseparable!&#8221;</p><footer>Second reply to Hayne, United States Senate, 1830 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12606/pg12606-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I wish to speak to-day, not as a Massachusetts man, nor as a Northern man, but as an American…&#8221;</p><footer>The Seventh of March speech, United States Senate, 1850 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12606/pg12606-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Secession! Peaceable secession! Sir, your eyes and mine are never destined to see that miracle. The dismemberment of this vast country without convulsion! The breaking up of the fountains of the great deep without ruffling the surface!&#8221;</p><footer>The Seventh of March speech, United States Senate, 1850 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12606/pg12606-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>1 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…and there was with them from that moment to the end of the war, what I hope will remain with them for ever, one cause, one country, one heart.&#8221;</p><footer>Address at the laying of the cornerstone of the Bunker Hill Monument, 1825 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12606/pg12606-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 539 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
